  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Infer the route's leg endpoints from successive changes of travel direction, including bypass points beside the ends of obstacles and arrivals at gaps. In these demonstrations a leg lasts approximately 25 control steps; useful endpoint times are approximately 0, 25, 50 and so on through the final frame. The frames straddle these times, so reconstruct the turning point from the adjacent incoming and outgoing trajectories rather than treating every displayed frame as a waypoint. Use the full video to distinguish retracing a bypass from continuing around the opposite end. Preserve all repetitions: reaching a previously visited gap does not mean the route is finished. Do not assume that all obstacles must be visited or that every traversal alternates sides.",
      "modify_to": "Identify the ordered legs and assign demonstration frames to their incoming and outgoing segments, including bypasses beside obstacle ends and arrivals at gaps. Approximate 25-control-step boundaries are segmentation aids only: a nearby displayed frame need not show an endpoint, and elapsed time must not be converted into distance by assuming constant speed. Reconstruct unsampled endpoints geometrically as described below. Use the full video to distinguish retracing a bypass from continuing around the opposite end. Preserve all repetitions as separate chronological occurrences; reaching a previously visited gap does not mean the route is finished. Do not force bypass turns on opposite-direction traversals to share coordinates merely because they pass the same obstacle end. Do not assume that all obstacles must be visited or that every traversal alternates sides."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Recover endpoint positions geometrically using the camera calibration and the tip's plane z=0.07 m. Intersect the ray through the lower free end with that plane; do not intersect the robot hand or upper end of the stick. When the tip is partially occluded, use neighboring demonstration frames, the visible shaft, obstacle geometry and the incoming/outgoing path to infer its endpoint. Ground-level marker centers must be reconstructed on their own ground plane rather than being treated as tip-height pixels. Use current front and wrist observations to refine alignment with the same static obstacle arrangement. Colors identify obstacles within the episode but do not define a universal route.",
      "modify_to": "For each usable demonstration frame, locate the lower free end of the stick, not the upper shaft, robot housing, marker or trail. Convert its pixel (u,v) into world coordinates using the camera calibration: C=-R^T t, d=R^T K^-1[u,v,1]^T, and X=C+((0.07-C_z)/d_z)d. Perform this ray-plane calculation rather than estimating metric offsets visually.\n\nFor an unsampled turn, fit the incoming and outgoing planar path directions from tip positions on their respective legs, using multiple frames wherever available. Extend those fitted segments to their intersection to recover the shared endpoint; with noisy observations, use a joint least-squares fit with a shared endpoint. Do not interpolate directly between the last incoming sample and first outgoing sample, which cuts across the corner. Those two samples alone do not determine two directions: use neighboring frames farther along each segment, clearly observed start or arrival positions, and obstacle geometry as additional constraints. For collinear reversals, where line intersection does not determine the endpoint, use observed arrival or repeated-visit evidence and gap geometry rather than inventing an intersection. Project the recovered endpoint back into the demonstration to check consistency with both segments and the demonstrated obstacle side. When the tip is occluded, use neighboring visible tip positions, shaft and trail evidence, and obstacle geometry as supporting constraints; do not substitute shaft or trail pixels into the tip-height projection.\n\nFor a gap arrival, distinguish the full arrival from a sample still approaching the gap. Locate the endpoint from incoming and outgoing path evidence, refining it with the marked gap center only when the marker's own plane is established. Recover the marker's planar coordinates on that plane, then set the commanded TCP height to 0.07 m; never project a ground-marker pixel onto the tip-height plane. The retained starting view or a clearly observed visit to the same gap can supply an additional localization constraint without merging separate route occurrences. For the final endpoint, prioritize the actual lower tip in the final demonstration frame, corroborated by preceding arrival frames, and project it onto z=0.07 m. Do not extrapolate beyond it to a nominal gap coordinate or nominal leg length.\n\nUse current front and wrist observations to refine alignment with the same static obstacle arrangement. Colors identify obstacles within the episode but do not define a universal route."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Command the demonstrated full leg endpoint, typically about 12 cm away, rather than inserting arbitrary small subdivisions, lifting over an obstacle, or moving directly across an obstacle to a later gap.",
      "modify_to": "Command the reconstructed absolute full leg endpoint, without coarse centimetre-scale rounding or added obstacle clearance. Neither a nominal leg length nor a fixed axial or diagonal offset defines that endpoint. Do not insert arbitrary small subdivisions, lift over an obstacle, or move directly across an obstacle to a later gap."
    }
  ],
  "refused": ""
}
